  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Employers are moving faster than most training is.</h2>
        <p>AI skills are now expected across a growing share of jobs, but the guidance available to teach them is unevenly matched to where people actually learn. Much of it was built for K–12 classrooms or narrow technical fields, leaving community colleges, universities, and workforce-training programs with few tools designed around how they teach and who they serve. Nexus Institute exists to close that specific gap.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="initiative">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The AI Education and Workforce Readiness Initiative</h2>
        <p>Five interconnected deliverables, developed as a single body of work rather than separate projects.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="deliv">
        <div class="deliv-item">
          <div class="deliv-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
          <div>
            <h3>AI Competency Frameworks and Standards</h3>
            <p>Defines the knowledge, skills, and judgment learners and workers need for effective, responsible AI use — across STEM and non-STEM fields alike.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="deliv-item">
          <div class="deliv-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Competency-Based Learning and Credentialing Systems</h3>
            <p>Turns defined competencies into structured learning experiences, authentic assessment, and stackable credentials that institutions can adopt directly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="deliv-item">
          <div class="deliv-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Validated Assessment and Measurement Systems</h3>
            <p>The Generic AI Competency (GAIC) Scale measures whether AI competency is actually developing, not just whether a course was completed.</p>
            <span class="status-note">Currently undergoing psychometric validation</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="deliv-item">
          <div class="deliv-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Global AI Education and Workforce Readiness Implementation Framework (GAEWRIF)</h3>
            <p>Explains how an institution builds the organizational capacity — leadership, governance, educator support — to sustain AI adoption at scale.</p>
            <span class="status-note">In active development</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="deliv-item">
          <div class="deliv-num" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Human–AI Instructional Design Operating System (IDOS)</h3>
            <p>Governs how the Institute's own instructional materials are designed and produced with AI, under continuous human oversight and review.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="research" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Research program</h2>
        <p>The Initiative is built on an evidence-generation cycle, not a one-time release.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="research-grid">
        <div class="research-card">
          <h3>Measurement research</h3>
          <p>The GAIC Scale's psychometric validation is being extended through a national labor-market study mapping employer demand for AI competencies across STEM and non-STEM industries, alongside a longer-term research trajectory extending the Scale's evidence base to community colleges, minority-serving institutions, and adult workforce programs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="research-card">
          <h3>Implementation science</h3>
          <p>GAEWRIF is being developed as an implementation-science research program studying how institutions convert organizational capability into curriculum, assessment, and educator practice — the mechanism most existing frameworks leave unspecified.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="research-note">
        <h3>Current scholarship</h3>
        <p>Current research extends into cross-sector qualitative studies of AI competency development and institutional capability across community college, university, and workforce settings, alongside a manuscript on institutional AI governance currently under peer review.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="stepper" role="list" aria-label="Operational lifecycle">
        <div class="step active" role="listitem"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Knowledge generation</div>
        <div class="step-sep" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="step" role="listitem"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Educational innovation</div>
        <div class="step-sep" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="step" role="listitem"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Field translation</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Who we serve</h2>
        <p>Initial work is grounded in underserved and Appalachian communities in Ohio, consistent with a Leave No One Behind approach — designing for the conditions that most challenge successful AI adoption before generalizing outward.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="serve-list">
        <span class="serve-pill">Community colleges</span>
        <span class="serve-pill">Universities</span>
        <span class="serve-pill">Workforce development organizations</span>
        <span class="serve-pill">Adult and continuing education programs</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="about" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>About the founder</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="about-grid">
        <p>Nexus Institute for AI Learning was founded by Joshua Osondu, a Ph.D. Candidate in Innovative Learning Design and Technology at Ohio University. His doctoral research developed the Generic AI Competency (GAIC) Scale, and his broader work integrates instructional systems design, educational measurement, implementation science, and AI governance to build the educational infrastructure institutions need for responsible AI adoption.</p>
        <p>Nexus Institute provides an independent organizational platform for continuing that work beyond any single research project or institutional appointment — developing, evaluating, and disseminating these tools for use across the field.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

